// ekf_range_bearing.eml -- Extended Kalman Filter measurement model for a
// range/bearing sensor observing a known landmark from a 2D robot pose
// (x, y, θ). The canonical EKF-SLAM / robot-localisation observation.
//
// ⚠ NAME COLLISION -- IF YOU CAME HERE FOLLOWING THE rb-EKF SILICON TRAIL,
// THIS IS THE WRONG FILE.
//
// A different kernel shares this filename. The reproduction trail (compiler-
// derived Jacobian -> normalised form -> certified fixed-point lowering ->
// emitted RTL -> silicon-capture replay, emission sha256 f47f6c81...) is built
// on a 2-D RADAR TARGET TRACKER:
//
//     track(x0, x1) -> (sqrt(x0*x0 + x1*x1), atan(x1 / x0))
//
// 28 lines, sha256 31c4eedd44307984... (LF-normalised). It lives at:
//
//     github.com/agent-maestro/machlib
//         reproduction/rb_ekf/kernel/ekf_range_bearing.eml
//     upstream: forge/examples/ekf_range_bearing.eml @ 18c3851
//
// THIS file models a robot pose (x, y, θ) against a landmark -- 60 lines, a
// different state vector, a different Jacobian, no silicon anchor. Emitting it
// will not reproduce f47f6c81..., and the mismatch will not tell you why.
//
// Flagged rather than renamed: this file is referenced by name elsewhere, and a
// rename would break working code to fix a documentation problem. Recorded
// 2026-08-01, when a reproduction dry run walked the trail as a stranger and
// found that searching public repositories for the source returns THIS file --
// a plausible wrong answer, with nothing erroring.
//
// A linear Kalman filter cannot use a range/bearing sensor: the measurement
//   h(x) = [ √(Δx² + Δy²) ,  atan2(Δy, Δx) − θ ]   (Δx = lx−x, Δy = ly−y)
// is NONLINEAR in the pose. The EXTENDED Kalman filter linearises it about
// the current estimate with the measurement Jacobian H = ∂h/∂x, then reuses
// the ordinary matrix update (see kalman2d_update_matrix.eml). Providing H
// is the whole job of the EKF measurement model -- that is what this file is.
//
// The Jacobian rows are RATIONAL in (Δx, Δy, r) -- no atan2 needed for the
// derivative even though the bearing measurement itself would be -- so they
// lower to plain scalar arithmetic every Forge backend emits:
//
//   ∂range/∂(x,y)   = [ −Δx/r ,  −Δy/r ]         (unit vector, landmark→robot)
//   ∂bearing/∂(x,y) = [  Δy/r² , −Δx/r² ]
//   ∂bearing/∂θ     = −1                          (constant; omitted here)
//
// VERIFIED vs NUMERICAL, stated honestly:
//   * `range` carries an @verify(lean) contract that the measured range is
//     non-negative (a Euclidean distance) -- proven sorryAx-free.
//   * The Jacobian rows are NUMERICALLY verified (cross-target vs a reference)
//     but carry no proof contract: they divide by r (and r²), so a sound
//     contract needs r>0 side conditions and division reasoning -- deferred,
//     not asserted.

module ekf_range_bearing;

// Range measurement: r = √((lx−x)² + (ly−y)²).  A Euclidean distance, so
// non-negative for ANY pose/landmark -- proven, not assumed.
@verify(lean, theorem = "ekf_range_nonneg")
fn range(x: Real, y: Real, lx: Real, ly: Real) -> Real
    where chain_order <= 0
    ensures (result >= 0.0)
{
    sqrt((lx - x)*(lx - x) + (ly - y)*(ly - y))
}

// Range row of the measurement Jacobian: ∂range/∂(x,y) = [−Δx/r, −Δy/r].
// The unit vector pointing from the landmark toward the robot. Requires a
// non-degenerate observation (robot not exactly on the landmark, r>0).
fn range_jacobian(x: Real, y: Real, lx: Real, ly: Real) -> Vec<2>
    where chain_order <= 0
    requires ((lx - x)*(lx - x) + (ly - y)*(ly - y) > 0.0)
{
    [(x - lx) / sqrt((lx - x)*(lx - x) + (ly - y)*(ly - y)),
     (y - ly) / sqrt((lx - x)*(lx - x) + (ly - y)*(ly - y))]
}

// Bearing row of the measurement Jacobian: ∂bearing/∂(x,y) = [Δy/r², −Δx/r²].
// Rational in the deltas -- the bearing derivative needs no atan2. Requires
// r²>0 (same non-degeneracy).
fn bearing_jacobian(x: Real, y: Real, lx: Real, ly: Real) -> Vec<2>
    where chain_order <= 0
    requires ((lx - x)*(lx - x) + (ly - y)*(ly - y) > 0.0)
{
    [(ly - y) / ((lx - x)*(lx - x) + (ly - y)*(ly - y)),
     (x - lx) / ((lx - x)*(lx - x) + (ly - y)*(ly - y))]
}
